+## PS1 TV frame-loop pacing (2026-09-08)
+
+The latest PID 17931 capture has working GPU presentation and correct PS1 audio
+batch size (`spf_in` and `spf_out` about 736). Heavy frames take 40–42 ms, while
+the loop adds roughly 3.6–3.7 ms of sleep. The sleep deadline was calculated
+from accumulators sampled before `retro_run`, excluding the current iteration's
+work. It also imposed a 1 ms sleep when emulation was already overdue.
+
+The sleep calculation now subtracts completed iteration work and returns zero
+when the next emulated frame is due. An overdue display-only deadline still
+waits for emulation, preserving the hot-spin fix; lifecycle gate refusal still
+backs off by a frame. Audio samples, the core clock and one achievement
+evaluation per emulated frame are unchanged.
+
+Host validation: frame-pacing policy, lifecycle, state-policy, presentation
+geometry and Vulkan chooser tests. Device validation remains required. This
+fix removes avoidable waiting; it does not establish 60 FPS or resolve the
+core's heavy-scene execution cost. Do not mask underproduction by discarding,
+repeating or fabricating audio frames.
 
